---- transcript ----
The invoice approval service is complete, and all 14 tests pass. Both `npm start` and the end-to-end curl flow were verified working.

## What was built

A Node.js + Express service backed by SQLite (via the built-in `node:sqlite` — no native build), with a minimal web UI and a requirement-by-requirement test suite.

**Files:**
- `server.js` — entry point (init DB, seed demo users, listen)
- `src/db.js` — schema, including **append-only audit triggers** that reject any UPDATE/DELETE
- `src/auth.js` — scrypt password hashing + auth/role middleware
- `src/audit.js`, `src/email.js` — audit helper and file-based email stub
- `src/app.js` — all routes and business rules
- `src/seed.js` — demo users
- `public/index.html` — minimal web UI (login, submit, filter, approve/reject)
- `test/invoices.test.js` — 14 tests, one per requirement
- `README.md` — setup, run, and test steps

## How each requirement is satisfied

| # | Requirement | Implementation |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Email/password, one role | Login endpoint, scrypt hashes, `role` column + `requireRole` |
| 2 | Invoice with PDF | `POST /api/invoices`, multer PDF-only filter |
| 3 | States, approver-only decisions | `submitted→approved\|rejected`, role-guarded |
| 4 | No self-approval | 403 when `submitter_id === approver.id` |
| 5 | Two approvers over €10k | `approvals` table with `UNIQUE(invoice_id, approver_id)`; threshold logic |
| 6 | Append-only audit log | Every state change recorded; DB triggers block edit/delete |
| 7 | Erasable, read-audited email | Erase endpoint + every read logged to audit |
| 8 | Filterable pending list | `/pending` filterable by counterparty/min/max amount |
| 9 | Email on state change | File-based stub (`emails/outbox.log` + per-message files) |
| 10 | Tests + README | `npm test` (14 tests) + `README.md` |

**To run:** `npm install` → `npm start` (serves on :3000, seeds demo users) → `npm test`. Demo accounts use password `password123`.

One note: I upgraded `multer` from the initially-installed 1.x to 2.x because npm flagged 1.x as having unpatched vulnerabilities.
